# QA verification discipline

A worker's summary of a sweep is not proof about a named instance, and neither is your own
note from this morning. Verify the instance, at the current tip, then report per item.

## Match the evidence to the kind of ask

A grep only ever proves a deletion.

| Ask | What counts as evidence |
|---|---|
| **Deletion** ("cut this sentence") | Grep the merged result and quote the literal output. |
| **New behaviour** (a border, a button, a visual state) | A test that fails without the change, or a screenshot of it rendering. Adding a style or a prop is not evidence that it renders. Where the claim is spatial, a passing test is not evidence either: see below. |
| **Bug fix** | A reproduction written first. If it cannot be made to fail, it cannot credibly be claimed fixed. "Probably already handled" is the tell that no reproduction exists. |
| **Rewrite** (prose quality) | You read the new prose against the original objection. This cannot be mechanised, and it is not the worker's to self-certify. |

## A spatial claim needs a frame, not a dimension

The new-behaviour row takes a test OR a screenshot. Where the claim is SPATIAL — where a
thing sits, how much room it leaves, what it covers — the test alone does not discharge
it, and neither does reading the computed box. A dimension is a claim about CSS; "can you
see the island behind it" is a claim about a rendered frame, and the first gets
substituted for the second because it is so much cheaper to obtain. A layout test can
assert the sheet is `72vw`, pass, and say nothing whatever about whether anything is
visible around it. So a spatial claim owes a rendered frame that someone LOOKED at, and
until there is one the claim is unverified, however exactly the box measures.

**The viewport is half the evidence.** The numbers under a spatial claim are usually
viewport-relative, so they resolve to different screens: `max-height: min(58vh, 480px)` is
480px on a tall monitor and about 294px in a 1024x580 browser with ~507px of usable
height. A frame taken at a comfortable developer viewport is therefore exactly as
misleading as the arithmetic, and misleading for the same reason — it will look fine. Take
spatial frames at a short, realistic viewport, **~1024x580**, and name the viewport beside
them, because a frame whose viewport is unstated says nothing about anybody's screen.

**`?dev` is not a fit-faithful surface, so the right viewport is not sufficient either.**
`WorldScene` gives the dev frame `size="full"`, and `.world-scene--full .coc-card` sets
`max-height: none` (`styles.css:4043`), overriding the cap a learner gets — `90vh` at
`styles.css:3910`, or `calc(100vh - 2 * var(--space-sm))` below 760px of viewport, which is
where any of this matters. Measured on one board at an identical 1024x580: the learner's lesson body
is 347px and the reviewer's is 409px, 18% more, with half as many controls pushed
off-screen. The comment at `styles.css:4022` says why — the cap *"cost a scroll on every
slide"* in dev, so it was lifted for reviewers and left in place for learners. So a frame
taken through `?dev` understates what a learner loses even when its viewport is short and
named, and every spatial claim ever checked there is owed a re-take on the learner's mount.
This is not a small correction: framed on the learner's surface, 0 of 15 gated widgets
sampled across nine domains fit at 1024x580, and one hid 644px of a 659px board.

**And the well is not one number: the pack's own title decides it.** Measured independently
over all ten domains on the learner's host at 1024x580 (`docs/qa-shell-fit-measurement.md`),
the 347px above reproduced exactly — and it was **300px**, 14% less, on the two packs whose
title wraps the banner to two lines, because a second title line costs 47px of card and the
card is capped. On one of those, POL.4, that left **15px** of a 483px board on screen. So a
widget that clears the number a rule quotes can still not fit the well two shipped packs
give it. This is a caution about the shape of the measurement rather than a second constant
to quote, because the constants move — and here they moved twice inside a week, in two lanes
at once. The short-viewport block at `styles.css:3961` trims the banner and clamps that title,
which took the same sweep to **369px on all ten** with the wrap gone; a separate lane's
`@media (max-height: 760px)` handed back the scrim gutter, and merged, the sweep reads
**411px on all ten**. Take the point rather than the number, and re-measure: a figure in a rule
is a measurement of a tree at a SHA, and the tree moves.

**"No width-pinned guard runs at this height" is a claim about the tree, so go and read it.**
A rule keyed on `max-height` looks like it cannot disturb a horizontal measurement, and that
holds only while nothing measures a width at a short viewport. Something does: a guard whose
viewport is **390x580** is width-pinned AND short, held short because vitest scales a tester
iframe it cannot fit, and it is exactly the pairing such a survey forgets. A short-screen block
written with `padding` shorthand — the shorthand sets the inline padding too, later in source
order than the `max-width: 720px` phone rule — took a 390px reading column from 330.8px to
306.8px and rewrapped a sentence a guard exists to keep on one line. Trim one axis with
`padding-block` / `padding-inline`, and before writing "nothing sees this", grep the viewports.

**A local green is not evidence when the measurement is text metrics.** That same defect
passed locally on Windows and failed deterministically on CI's fonts, because a 24px loss
rewraps a line here and not there. Where a claim rests on where text lands, a local pass buys
you very little and the CI run is the reading; do not push it as verified and do not, when it
goes red, reach for "transient" before reproducing it. It reproduces under injection at the
same width if it is real.

**A claim about what is visible BEHIND or AROUND an element has to be framed to show the
other thing.** Cropped to the element that changed, a screenshot proves nothing about
occlusion: the element is the one part of the picture nobody doubted. The subject of the
evidence is the surface, not the component.

**A frame is only as faithful as its MOUNT, and a bare mount is missing the theme.** The
panel tokens (`--glass-fill-strong` and its neighbours) are declared on `.city-shell`
(`styles.css:1915`), not at `:root`, so a component mounted straight onto `document.body`
resolves them to nothing and every panel that paints with one comes out TRANSPARENT. A frame
taken there shows content sliding through boxes that are opaque for every learner, which
reads as a defect and is not one — and the reverse trap is the one that matters, since a
genuinely transparent overlay would look no different. Mount inside `.city-shell`, or say in
the report which tokens the frame is missing.

**A width pinned in a guard is the same substitution with a test standing in for the
box.** A dozen layout files measure at `FIGURE_CONTENT_CAP` (460px), which is a stylesheet
cap on figure CONTENT and not a container anything ships; the lesson column a learner and
a `?dev` reviewer are actually given is 920px, and a 390px phone gives 305px. That gap is
deliberate and `readingWidths.ts` holds the ruling — 460 is a defensible mid-range spot
check, and widening it would LOSE the wrap coverage, since nothing wraps at 894 — so this
is a caution about how to read those files, not a licence to repin them. Read green at a
pinned width as what it is: a true measurement in a box no container has, which buys no
more than a correct dimension over an island nobody can see.

## Changing a guard owes the same evidence as a fix

The bug-fix row asks for a failure before a fix. The same failure is owed when the thing
you changed is the guard. Loosening an assertion to make a feature possible may well
leave it catching the defect it was written for, but that is an argument, and an
assertion exists so that nobody has to take the argument on trust: inject that defect and
watch it fail at the loosened lines. An assertion you add without a demonstrated red is
not evidence either, because a test written green may be asserting nothing. And a
containment or subset assertion needs a floor: containment holds trivially over an empty
collection, so with no count behind it the assertion survives the emptiness it appeared
to rule out.

## Scope a guard by the behaviour, not by the implementation

**Define a guard's population by who exhibits the behaviour, not by where the behaviour is
implemented.** Sharing an implementation is a fact about the code. Exhibiting a behaviour
is a fact about the product, and the second is what the guard is about. So the question to
answer before writing the population down is not "where does this mechanic live" but "what
would have to be true of a surface for this defect to be possible there", because a
surface can carry a mechanic without importing a line of it.

A reveal that destroyed the answers a learner had not yet given reached eighteen boards
this way, while the guard written for exactly that defect passed every run.
`revealReAsk.test.ts` required the `othersRemain` prop from the seven widgets built on a
shared kind, on the reasoning that a shared archetype holds several answers at once. That
reasoning was correct, and it was equally true of nineteen bespoke widgets holding several
answers at once behind their own copies of the mechanic. Scoped to the abstraction the
guard was narrower than the behaviour, so every widget that reimplemented the mechanic
fell outside it. Measured rather than argued, at `6bdb318`: strip the prop from one
bespoke widget, run the guard exactly as it shipped, and the file reports **114 passed, 0
failed** over a source containing zero occurrences of the string it checks. Widen the
population on that same tree and it is 1 failed, 132 passed (`#219`).

**A derived population with a floor is not sufficient, because it can derive the wrong
set.** `lesson-qa`'s trap list says do not pin a hardcoded count: derive the population
from source and floor it. That is right, and this guard obeyed every word of it. Its
population was derived, from the sources, with a floor under it, and it was still the
wrong set. **Deriving correctly is a separate question from deriving at all**, and a
derivation is at its most convincing when it is one clean filter over a real source, which
is also exactly what a too-narrow filter looks like.

**A floor constrains size, not shape.** A floor of 26 is satisfied by the original seven
plus nineteen of anything, so a population can drift back to the wrong composition and
still clear it. Where the composition is the claim, assert the composition: beside its
floor, `revealReAsk.test.ts` asserts that the population with the shared kinds removed is
itself at least nineteen, which puts "this sweep is genuinely wider than the layer it used
to stop at" somewhere it can fail.

**This is not a hypothesis about how guards go wrong.** Thirteen guards in this tree were
found reporting green while measuring nothing or the wrong thing over 2026-07-28 and
2026-07-29: six sweeps whose coverage assertion counted their own written-down list
instead of the tree, two of those already false (`#191`); a validator rule whose
population had been empty since a design change removed all 63 of its inputs (`#193`);
eleven error-tier content rules never wired into CI, so a green run covered none of them
(`#198`); a silence assertion satisfied by a counter that never moves, green over 43 cases
under a fully muted audio context (`#207`); `tsc --build` exiting 0 over a config file no
project includes (`#215`); a placeholder cohort table free to drift off the dispatch it
mirrors (`#217`); a prose rule whose one shape could not match the sentence it was written
for (`#187`); and the population above (`#219`). Read that as closed history for two dates
rather than as a live figure. What it is evidence for is the rate: this is the ordinary
way a guard fails here, not an exotic one, so the population is worth an argument every
time you write one.

## A legitimate exception is a named list that fails both ways

Some members of a correctly scoped population genuinely do not owe the property the guard
requires. A member that does not owe it and a member that forgot it look identical from
outside the guard, so quietly leaving the first out of the population is how the second
ships. Name them instead, each with its reason, and assert the list in **both**
directions:

- an entry that stops qualifying fails, so a renamed or deleted member cannot sit in the
  list forever exempting nothing and hiding that it exempts nothing;
- a qualifier that adopts the property without leaving the list fails, so the list cannot
  go on excusing something it has stopped describing.

The second direction is what stops an exception becoming permanent by neglect. The two
together are also what make an exemption provable rather than assumed: force one false,
and the sweep fails at the member it was excusing. That is a one-line injection, and it is
the only way to tell a load-bearing exemption from a decorative one.

Three separate agents converged on this shape over 2026-07-28 and 2026-07-29, so take the
worked example from one of them rather than inventing it again. All three read at
`6bdb318`:

- **`apps/web/src/lessons/revealReAsk.test.ts` (`#219`) is the worked example**, and the
  fullest of the three: two lists, because they are two different claims
  (`NEVER_HANDS_BACK` for the surfaces that hold one answer, `HAND_BACK_OWED_IN_D1` for
  boards owing the property in another owner's lane), a disjointness check so nothing is
  claimed as both, and a floor that fails if the lists grow until they have swallowed the
  population. Forcing one entry out of `NEVER_HANDS_BACK` fails at *"./cfu.tsx tells the
  shared layer what is left of its board"*, which is an exemption naming what it excuses; a
  listed widget that adopts the property fails at *"has not quietly adopted the flag"*; a
  listed path that no longer exists fails at *"is a declared exception that still exists
  and still offers a reveal"*.
- `apps/web/src/lessons/widgetSuccessCoverage.test.ts` (`UNJUDGED`, `#191`) reads each
  excused widget's source instead of counting the list, so an excuse plus a badge is a
  stale excuse.
- `apps/web/src/lessons/answerFormat.test.ts` (`UNREACHED`, `#188`) exempts named
  placeholders from a count floor, with a set-equality census that fails whichever way the
  list and the tree come apart.

## Verify the instance, not the class

"I swept for this class of defect" and "I edited this sentence" are different claims. When
the user quotes a string, that string is the test. Check it at the merged SHA, not in a
branch and not in a diff.

## A finding expires when the tree moves

A finding is evidence about a SHA, not about the repository. File:line citations are the
clearest case: their precision is what makes them feel durable, and one merge in that
area voids all of them at once. So before acting on anything recorded earlier, your own
inventory as much as a worker's report, re-read it at the current tip. If a merge has
landed in that area since it was recorded, it is unverified until re-checked, however
carefully it was verified the first time. The costs are not symmetric: re-reading a
finding costs a minute, and briefing a worker on a stale one verbatim costs their whole
run.

When two of your own records disagree about whether something is done, that contradiction
is the finding. A COMPLETED entry and a still-open one covering the same work cannot both
be current, so resolve them against the tree before doing anything else. Do not let the
older entry win by being longer or more detailed than the newer one: it reads as more
authoritative because it was written while the work was still open.

## Report item-by-item

Turn a QA list into a numbered checklist, each item naming its evidence type before work
starts. Report back per item with that evidence attached, and an explicit **NOT DONE** for
anything skipped. Never answer one specific line with a paragraph about the general class:
prose hides the omission that per-item structure exposes.

## Never report done on a summary alone

If it is unverified, say **"claimed but unverified."** Merge state included: read the PR's
`mergedAt` rather than inferring from pushes, because a push to a shared branch may be
someone else's merge. Read it before describing what a PR has done to the codebase, too,
because a PR's contents are not the tree's contents. "#N un-gated these two nodes,"
written while #N is open, is a true statement about the diff and a false one about
integration, where the files still record the gate: which is usually why you were reading
them.

Reviewing a lesson domain: `.cursor/skills/lesson-qa/SKILL.md` owns the methodology
(invoke it explicitly, it does not auto-load). This rule owns what you are allowed to
claim.
